### Workbook structure and caching

The toolkit caches the most recently supplied LOE template's structure at
`C:\Users\d-jarrett.booz\.claude\assisted-delivery\cache\loe-workbook\baseline.xlsm`
and `...\cache\loe-workbook\baseline-structure.json`.

- If the user supplied a template path this run:
  1. Parse it:
     ```
     "C:\Users\d-jarrett.booz\.claude\assisted-delivery\node_modules\.bin\tsx.cmd" "C:\Users\d-jarrett.booz\.claude\assisted-delivery\src\parse-loe-workbook.ts" "<template.xlsm>"
     ```
  2. If `baseline-structure.json` already exists, diff the two:
     ```
     "C:\Users\d-jarrett.booz\.claude\assisted-delivery\node_modules\.bin\tsx.cmd" "C:\Users\d-jarrett.booz\.claude\assisted-delivery\src\diff-loe-workbook.ts" "<cached-structure.json>" "<fresh-structure.json>"
     ```
  3. For every structural change that could change a scoping decision —
     a new or renamed functional group that might map to the engagement,
     a modified Simple/Medium/Complex definition on a row you're about to
     size, a shifted column role — ask the user about it **one question
     at a time**, in plain language, before finalizing decisions that
     touch it. Do not silently guess through drift.
  4. After scoping is complete, overwrite both cache files with this
     run's template bytes and parsed structure, so the next run (with no
     template supplied) picks up this version.
- If the user did not supply a template path this run, read the cached
  `baseline-structure.json` (and keep `baseline.xlsm` as the source for
  any filled-copy output). If neither cache file exists, stop and ask the
  user to supply the LOE template once.

## Scoping-decision discipline (never guess)

The template spans ~20+ unrelated ServiceNow product families across
hundreds of rows (App Engine, BCM, CSM, EA, ITSM, SPM, HR, FSM, Platform
Portal, Telecommunications Service Management, ...). **Only emit a
decision for a feature row when there is a citable basis in the
Requirements Register:**

- An **in-scope** citation (a requirement that names, or clearly implies,
  that feature or its functional group) → `decision: "in_scope"`,
  `evidenceBasis: "stated"`.
- A row that follows from a stated requirement by necessity or standard
  bundling, without being named itself → `decision: "in_scope"`,
  `evidenceBasis: "derived"` (see "Derived scope" below). This still
  counts as a citable basis — it is never a substitute for one.
- An **explicit out-of-scope statement** in the register (e.g. "ITAM is
  out of scope," "ITOM/ITAM and CMDB configuration is out of scope,"
  "Mobile apps are out of scope," "Virtual Agent is out of scope") that
  covers that feature or functional group → `decision: "out_of_scope"`,
  `evidenceBasis: "stated"` (exclusions are always stated, never derived).

A functional group with **no** requirement evidence either way (BCM, EA,
HR, FSM, Communities, and similarly unmentioned groups) gets **no
decisions at all** — leave every one of its feature rows unaddressed and
report the whole group in a `warnings` entry ("no requirement evidence
either way; left unmarked rather than guessed"). Never fill a confident
"No" purely because a product wasn't mentioned — that is exactly the kind
of unsupported guess this agent exists to avoid. This mirrors
`assisted-delivery-requirements-extractor`'s own rule: never substitute a
guess for missing evidence.

## Derived scope — architecting beyond the literal citation

Scoping a ServiceNow engagement is not pure text-matching — a competent
architect routinely extends a stated requirement to the other rows a
reasonable implementation of that requirement cannot do without. Apply
`evidenceBasis: "derived"` in exactly two situations, each requiring a
`citationIds` pointing at the triggering **stated** requirement plus a
`derivationRationale` spelling out the chain ("X is stated → Y is a
necessary/standard part of delivering X → therefore Y is in scope"):

- **Technical necessity** — ServiceNow cannot satisfy the stated
  requirement without this row. Example: a stated requirement for
  "bi-directional schedule synchronization into Outlook" necessitates a
  calendar/notification integration row even if that row's name never
  appears in the register.
- **Standard bundling** — the row is a normal sub-component of a
  capability that IS explicitly stated. Example: a stated "ITSM
  implementation" licenses an `in_scope`/`derived` decision on baseline
  ITSM-Overall/Reports/Request/Release rows, not just the ITSM modules the
  register happens to enumerate by name. Example: a stated "basic portal
  implementation" licenses itemizing Portal Branding, Portal Pages, and
  Portal Widgets as separate derived rows instead of folding them silently
  into one Service Portal line.
- **Platform baseline plumbing** — a row that *any* real-world build of
  the stated overall engagement scope and scale cannot go live without,
  even though no single named capability in the register calls it out by
  name. The triggering "stated requirement" here is the engagement's own
  overall scope statement (e.g. "ITSM implementation," "SPM
  implementation") plus whatever scale signals the register gives
  (headcount, directorate/business-unit count, record volumes) — not a
  specific line item. Treat these as standing derivation targets whenever
  their trigger conditions are met, the same way you'd treat Standard
  bundling:
  - Instance/base platform setup (instance provisioning, basic system
    settings) — triggered by any stated implementation at all; every
    build needs this once.
  - Core foundational data setup (locations, departments, cost centers,
    companies) — triggered when the register states more than one
    business unit/directorate/site that process configuration must span.
  - Platform-level notification/email infrastructure (SMTP/mail-server
    setup, as distinct from any single process's own notification
    *content*, which stays on that process's own row) — triggered by any
    stated implementation that includes Incident/Request/Change-style
    processes.
  - Inbound email-to-record intake — triggered by a stated
    Incident/Request implementation, as a standard production-grade
    intake channel.
  - Document/attachment management — triggered when the register
    describes unstructured documents, scanned approvals, or attachments
    that must be retained or migrated.
  - On-call/schedule rotation — triggered when the register states
    sustained staffing for incident operations (headcount, shift
    coverage, "22 Tier 1 staff," etc.) that would realistically need
    after-hours coverage.
  - SLA/Service Level Management — triggered by a stated "full lifecycle"
    or production-grade Incident/Request implementation.
  - Agent workspace — triggered by a stated Incident/Problem/Change build
    serving named operational staff.
  - A generic flow/automation catch-all row (e.g. "Flow Designer, per
    flow") — triggered when multiple *other* in-scope rows each imply
    their own distinct piece of custom trigger/notification/alert logic
    (e.g. a 180-day contract-expiry alert, a firewall-request routing
    notification, a calendar-sync trigger) that doesn't belong to any one
    of those rows' own definition. Count one unit per distinct
    cross-cutting flow identified this way, cited back to each flow's own
    triggering row.
  Every platform-baseline-plumbing decision still needs a
  `derivationRationale` naming the specific scale/scope signal that
  triggered it (not just "every implementation needs this" with no
  citation) and a citation to that signal.

**Avoid double-counting the same volume/complexity driver across two
rows.** When one stated volume number (an article count, a record count)
could plausibly inflate more than one row — e.g. a base
module-activation row (`Knowledge Management`) and a separate per-
import/per-count row for the same subject (`Knowledge Article Import`) —
apply that volume to the row whose own Simple/Medium/Complex definition
text is actually volume/count-driven, and keep the companion base-
activation row at its baseline tier (do not promote both). If you catch
yourself citing the same number as the reason for two different rows'
tier assignment, stop and reconsider which one it actually belongs to.

**Before concluding a stated volume has "no corresponding Module Scope
row," check for a generic bulk-import/data-load catch-all row first.**
Stated migration/population volumes (user counts, incident/case counts,
total data size) almost always map onto either (a) a product-specific
"... Import (per source/import)" row already counted elsewhere, or (b) a
generic platform-level bulk-import row (commonly named something like
"IntegrationHub Import" or folded into "User & Role Management" for user
population imports). Search the parsed workbook structure for rows whose
`feature` text contains "Import," "Migration," or "Bulk" before reporting
a stated volume as unmapped in `warnings` — only report it as having no
matching row after that search comes back empty.

**Non-examples — do not derive these:**
- Never derive a row when there is no stated trigger at all anywhere in
  the register (that's the "no evidence either way" case — leave it
  unaddressed, don't derive it from general ServiceNow convention). A
  platform-baseline-plumbing row still needs its own named trigger
  (above) — "ServiceNow implementations generally need this" alone is not
  a citation.
- Never use derivation to override or soften an explicit out-of-scope
  statement.
- Derivation decides **scope** (is this row Yes/No), never **size** — see
  the sizing-basis rule below for how an in-scope derived row still gets
  its unit count.

## Sizing judgment (Simple / Medium / Complex units)

For every `in_scope` decision (stated or derived), first try to compare
the Requirements Register's stated scale and volume language against that
feature row's Simple/Medium/Complex definition text (the template's
`simple_definition`/`medium_definition`/`complex_definition` columns) to
choose unit counts, and set `sizingBasis: "stated"`. Example: a
requirement citing "3,760 knowledge articles" against a Knowledge
Management feature row whose Simple definition caps at a lower article
count and whose Complex definition's cap covers that volume should size
toward Complex, with the rationale naming the specific numbers compared.

When the register gives **no** volume/count language for that row at all,
**Disclosed benchmark sizing is mandatory, not optional.** A `Yes`/
`in_scope` decision must never end up with all-zero Simple/Medium/Complex
units — a human preparer scanning the filled workbook needs to see a
number on every row marked in scope, exactly like a competent solution
architect would produce by hand:

- **Disclosed benchmark** — size the row from a reasonable, named
  industry-standard figure for this kind of row (e.g. "a typical Day-1
  ITSM catalog for an organization this size runs 20-50 items; using 25
  as a conservative mid-point"), with `sizingBasis: "benchmark"` and a
  `benchmarkRationale` that names the convention used and explicitly
  states it is **not** sourced from the client's own documentation, so
  the human preparer can spot and override every non-client-sourced
  number at a glance. Reach for this on every in-scope row with a named,
  recognizable ServiceNow feature (Incident Management, Change
  Management, Problem Management, catalog item builds, reporting, etc.)
  — there is almost always a defensible mid-point convention for a named
  ITSM/SPM/Portal feature even without a client-stated count.
- **Ambiguous zero-unit sizing is a near-never escape hatch, not a
  co-equal alternative to benchmarking.** Do not reach for it merely
  because the register lacks a client-stated number — that is exactly
  what Disclosed benchmark exists for. Only use it when the feature is so
  idiosyncratic to this client that no industry convention could possibly
  apply, and your `ambiguityNote` must say specifically why a benchmark
  doesn't apply (not just that one wasn't given). If you cannot
  articulate that, you have not actually exhausted the benchmark option —
  go back and benchmark it. And if a row has no citable in-scope basis at
  all, the right move is to leave it **unaddressed** (see the "no
  decisions at all" rule above), never to mark it in-scope and leave it
  unsized.

Never silently blend a benchmark number into the same bucket as a stated
one — the sizing basis tag is what lets a human preparer trust the sheet
without re-deriving it themselves.

## Artifacts

**Output composition — avoid the per-turn output cap.** A template this
size can produce hundreds of decisions. Never compose the entire HTML or
JSON in one tool call. Build both incrementally, across many separate
turns, exactly as `assisted-delivery-requirements-extractor` does, always
using the `Write`/`Edit` tools directly against the final allocated path
from the very first write — never stage output in a scratch/temp
directory (e.g. `C:\tmp\...`, `%TEMP%\...`) and then shell out to `cp` to
move it into place, and never use a `Bash` heredoc (`cat > file <<EOF`)
to produce a deliverable. The `Write`/`Edit` tools are already permitted
against every allocated output directory, so there is never a reason to
route a deliverable through a raw filesystem copy — doing so only adds
unnecessary permission prompts for `cp`/`rm`/`cat >`, which this toolkit
never pre-approves because they are generic mutating operations. The one
exception is genuine scratch work you create and consume entirely for
your own reasoning (e.g. a throwaway comparison script) and never deliver
to the user — that may live under `C:\tmp\` and should be cleaned up with
the `Write`/`Edit` tools or left behind, not deleted via `Bash rm -rf`:

- Write an HTML skeleton first (title, headings for every required
  section), then add each section's content with its own separate `Edit`
  call — batch the Scoping Decisions table by functional group rather
  than writing all rows in one call.
- `Write` a minimal valid JSON skeleton first (identity, empty
  `sourceInventory: []`, `documents: []`, `citations: []`,
  `workbookStructure` populated once known, `structuralChanges: []`,
  `decisions: []`, `assumptions: []`, `questions: []`, `warnings: []`),
  then `Read` + `Edit` to append each functional group's decisions and
  citations in its own separate call.
- Keep your own conversational responses compact throughout — report
  paths and short summaries; never restate the full HTML or JSON content
  back into a message.

Allocate synchronized `level-of-effort` HTML, JSON, and (when producing a
filled workbook copy) `xlsm` paths by running (never overwrite an
existing version):

```
"C:\Users\d-jarrett.booz\.claude\assisted-delivery\node_modules\.bin\tsx.cmd" "C:\Users\d-jarrett.booz\.claude\assisted-delivery\src\allocate-artifact-paths.ts" --application "<application>" --request "<request>" --base level-of-effort --extension html --extension json --extension xlsm
```

Write one self-contained HTML report at the allocated path, presenting
these sections in exact order:

1. Summary — counts by decision (in/out of scope), total Simple/Medium/
   Complex units, the count of ambiguous decisions, and the stated-vs-
   derived split of in-scope decisions and the stated-vs-benchmark split
   of sizing bases.
2. Source Documents — the Requirements Register used and the LOE
   template version (`templateFileName` / `templateFingerprint`) scored
   against.
3. Structural Changes Since Last Baseline — every entry from
   `structuralChanges`, or an explicit statement that none were detected
   (including when no prior baseline existed to diff against).
4. Scoping Decisions — grouped by functional group, one row per decision:
   feature, decision (Yes/No), evidence basis (Stated/Derived), Simple/
   Medium/Complex units, sizing basis (Stated/Benchmark, when units are
   nonzero), comment, rationale, citation.
4a. Derived & Benchmark-Based Decisions — For Human Review: every row
    where `evidenceBasis === "derived"` or `sizingBasis === "benchmark"`,
    rolled up in one place with its full `derivationRationale` and/or
    `benchmarkRationale`, so a human preparer can scan and veto every
    non-literally-cited number without hunting through section 4.
5. Unaddressed Functional Groups — every functional group with no
   decisions at all, and why.
6. Assumptions.
7. Open Questions.
8. Warnings and Limitations — explicitly disclose that Excel may show
   stale `O`/`K` formula values in a filled copy until the user forces a
   recalculation (F9).

Write the `LevelOfEffortPackage` JSON at the same allocated version, with
`artifacts.html`/`artifacts.json`/`artifacts.xlsm` set to the exact
allocated paths.

If producing the filled workbook copy, build the decisions array into a
temporary JSON file and run:

```
"C:\Users\d-jarrett.booz\.claude\assisted-delivery\node_modules\.bin\tsx.cmd" "C:\Users\d-jarrett.booz\.claude\assisted-delivery\src\fill-loe-workbook.ts" "<source-xlsm>" "<structure.json>" "<decisions.json>" "<allocated-xlsm-path>"
```

Never pass the user's original template as `<allocated-xlsm-path>` — the
output path must always be a brand-new file.

**Workbook-fill invariant.** The filled workbook leaves every
`out_of_scope` row completely blank — no literal `"No"` text, no zero
units, no comment. The toolkit's `fillLoeWorkbook`/`buildCellWrites`
already enforce this (they emit no cell writes at all for an
`out_of_scope` decision), but you must never work around it or
post-process the output to add "No" markers back in. Only `in_scope` rows
receive any cell writes in the workbook. `out_of_scope` decisions remain
fully visible in the HTML/JSON report for traceability — the workbook and
the report are allowed to differ in this one respect by design.


Keep reusable instructional content client-neutral using `CLIENT NAME`/
`APPLICATION NAME`/`INSTANCE NAME` placeholders only in examples or
instructions — every delivered artifact must use the real client,
application, and template names, never placeholders.

Validate the JSON and its bound HTML (and, when present, the filled
`.xlsm`) by running:

```
"C:\Users\d-jarrett.booz\.claude\assisted-delivery\node_modules\.bin\tsx.cmd" "C:\Users\d-jarrett.booz\.claude\assisted-delivery\src\validate-assisted-loe.ts" "<absolute-json-path>"
